evidence:
Ran against public/wasm/sql-wasm.js (sql.js 1.14.1 / SQLite 3.49.1 — the same WASM the lesson player and teach-demos.test.ts use), seeded with this lesson's own demoSeedSql (level2.ts:1725-1735):
  CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, total_cents INTEGER);
  INSERT INTO orders VALUES (100,1000),(101,2000),(102,9000),(103,3000),(104,500);

All three positions a scalar subquery can occupy — NO ERROR in any of them:
  SELECT order_id FROM orders WHERE total_cents > (SELECT total_cents FROM orders);
    -> NO ERROR, returned: [[101],[102],[103]]      (silently used the first row's 1000)
  SELECT order_id, (SELECT total_cents FROM orders) AS sub FROM orders LIMIT 2;
    -> NO ERROR, returned: [[100,1000],[101,1000]]
  SELECT order_id FROM orders WHERE total_cents = (SELECT total_cents FROM orders);
    -> NO ERROR, returned: [[100]]

The quoted message is the multi-COLUMN error, not a multi-row error, and this build does not even emit that text:
  SELECT order_id FROM orders WHERE total_cents > (SELECT order_id, total_cents FROM orders);
    -> ERROR: row value misused

Ran against the set-ops lesson's own demoSeedSql (level2.ts:1574-1588), which contains exactly the situation the prose describes — EU order 101 and US order 201 both have total_cents = 5000:
  INSERT INTO orders_eu VALUES (100,2500),(101,5000),(102,9900);
  INSERT INTO orders_us VALUES (200,1500),(201,5000);

Using the demo's own projection (order_id, total_cents):
  SELECT order_id, total_cents FROM orders_eu UNION ALL SELECT order_id, total_cents FROM orders_us;
    -> [[100,2500],[101,5000],[102,9900],[200,1500],[201,5000]]   (5 rows)
  SELECT order_id, total_cents FROM orders_eu UNION     SELECT order_id, total_cents FROM orders_us;
    -> [[100,2500],[101,5000],[102,9900],[200,1500],[201,5000]]   (5 rows — IDENTICAL; nothing was dropped)

Swapping UNION for UNION ALL changes nothing, directly contradicting "Reaching for UNION by reflex silently drops legitimate duplicate rows" as illustrated by that parenthetical.

Line 50 asserts: "The bare keyword `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` is the one exception that needs no parentheses." Ran in sqlite3:
  CREATE TABLE t2 (a TEXT DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE, b TEXT DEFAULT CURRENT_TIME);
  SELECT name, dflt_value FROM pragma_table_info('t2');
  -> a|CURRENT_DATE
     b|CURRENT_TIME
Both are accepted bare, so there are three exceptions, not one. (The lesson's neighbouring claim IS correct and I confirmed it: `CREATE TABLE bad (x TEXT DEFAULT datetime('now'));` -> "Parse error ... near \"(\": syntax error".)

$ python3 (sqlite3, 400k rows, users 0-999, 4 statuses)
  CREATE INDEX ix_uid_created_status ON orders(user_id, created_at, status);
  EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN SELECT id,total FROM orders WHERE user_id=1 AND status='paid' ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;
  -> (5, 0, 0, 'SEARCH orders USING INDEX ix_uid_created_status (user_id=?)')
  contains TEMP B-TREE / sort step?: False

No "USE TEMP B-TREE FOR ORDER BY" appears: the sort is free. Contrast the recommended order, which additionally pins status:
  CREATE INDEX ix_uid_status_created ON orders(user_id, status, created_at);
  -> 'SEARCH orders USING INDEX ix_uid_status_created (user_id=? AND status=?)'

Within a user_id the entries are still sorted by created_at, so the ordering is index-provided either way; the real penalty is that the engine must walk and discard other statuses before it collects 20 rows.
